      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">1. Gor'kov Acoustic Radiation Potential & Levitational Force</h4>
        <p>
          Single-axis ultrasonic standing waves create alternating spatial planes of acoustic pressure nodes (minimum pressure oscillation, maximum velocity) and antinodes (maximum pressure oscillation). The time-averaged acoustic radiation force acting on a spherical liquid droplet in an arbitrary acoustic field is governed by the negative gradient of the Gor'kov potential:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px] text-sky-300 border border-hairline/50">
          U = 2π r₀³ [ (⟨p²⟩ / 3ρ₀c₀²) · f₁ - (ρ₀⟨v²⟩ / 2) · f₂ ]<br/>
          F_rad = -∇U
        </div>
        <p>
          where f₁ = 1 - κ_p/κ₀ (compressibility factor) and f₂ = 2(ρ_p - ρ₀)/(2ρ_p + ρ₀) (density factor). For dense liquid droplets in air (ρ_p ≫ ρ₀), f₂ ≈ 1 and f₁ ≈ 1, generating powerful acoustic forces (F_rad ≥ m·g) that stably hold the liquid suspended in mid-air against gravity without any physical container or support.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">2. Schlichting Boundary Layer Streaming & Enhanced Evaporation</h4>
        <p>
          Unlike quiescent air drying where a saturated stagnant vapor blanket envelopes the droplet, intense standing wave acoustics induce steady, non-zero time-averaged fluid vortices known as Rayleigh and Schlichting acoustic streaming. Viscous dissipation in the acoustic Stokes boundary layer (Stokes layer δ_v = √(2ν/ω)) generates powerful toroidal circulating jets with slip velocities:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px] text-sky-300 border border-hairline/50">
          u_streaming ≈ (3 / 8) · (⟨p²⟩ / (ρ₀² c₀³))<br/>
          Sh = 2 + 0.55 · Re_streaming^(1/2) · Sc^(1/3)
        </div>
        <p>
          These continuous toroidal micro-eddies sweep away boundary moisture, boosting mass transfer by 250% to 400% compared to classical diffusion-limited evaporation, enabling room-temperature drying at rates comparable to thermal ovens.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">3. Droplet Aspect Ratio (a/b) & Acoustic Atomization Thresholds</h4>
        <p>
          Acoustic radiation pressure is non-uniform across the droplet surface, exerting maximum normal stress at the poles and negative stress at the equator. This deforms the droplet from a sphere into an oblate spheroid (a/b &gt; 1). The balance between surface tension σ and acoustic pressure determines droplet stability:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px] text-sky-300 border border-hairline/50">
          We = (ρ_g · u_streaming² · d₀) / σ ≤ We_crit ≈ 10 - 12<br/>
          (a/b)_max ≈ 3.2 (Instability threshold)
        </div>
        <p>
          If sound pressure level (SPL) is increased beyond the critical threshold, surface capillary waves undergo parametric Faraday instability, causing catastrophic liquid ring ejection or acoustic atomization. Precise transducer power modulation keeps the Weber number below 10 for stable, controlled drying.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">4. Zero-Wall Fouling & 99% Enzyme Tertiary Structure Retention</h4>
        <p>
          In conventional spray dryers or scraped-surface evaporators, high nozzle shear (&gt;10,000 s⁻¹) and thermal collisions with stainless steel walls cause denaturing of tertiary protein folding, Maillard scorch, and sticky wall deposits (15%–35% yield loss). In containerless acoustic levitation:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-muted">
          <li><strong>Wall Contact = 0%:</strong> 100% elimination of sticky deposits, scorch, and heterogeneous crystallization.</li>
          <li><strong>Zero Shear Stress:</strong> Liquid experiences gentle internal recirculating laminar flow without shear damage to complex quaternary proteins (IgG, β-galactosidase).</li>
          <li><strong>Isothermal Operation (15–25°C):</strong> Evaporation occurs without boiling, preserving 98.5% to 99.5% of native enzymatic activity and fragile volatile esters.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
